# Hostile audit — diameter-thrackle / dual-Helly common extended cell

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Reviewed:** `research/target_c_diameter_thrackle_dual_helly_cell_2026-09-15.md`  
**Disposition:** **PASS after the collar-extension repair, at proof-only structural scope.**  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The first draft had one real scope gap: it treated the audited train disk as if it were
convex, so an actual straight diameter segment was assumed to remain inside that disk.
That is not guaranteed.  The revised theorem fixes this by extending every proper train
crosscut through a collar to a large convex ambient disk.  The audit below checks that
this repair preserves both the dual tree and the literal straight-chord geometry.

---

## 1. Planar diameter segments really form a geometric thrackle

For a diameter pair `ab`, the inequality

\[
|x-b|^2\le|a-b|^2
\]

implies

\[
(x-a)\cdot(b-a)\ge|x-a|^2/2.
\]

Thus `a` is exposed, and similarly every diameter endpoint is exposed.  Four distinct
endpoints belonging to two diameter pairs are therefore in strict convex position.

If the two diameter segments were disjoint, they would be opposite sides of that
convex quadrilateral.  The two diagonals cross at an interior point `o`, and strict
triangle inequality gives

\[
|ab|+|cd|<|ac|+|bd|.
\]

The left side is `2D`, so one diagonal would exceed the diameter.  Contradiction.

Shared endpoints are already intersections.  Collinear-disjoint degeneracy cannot
occur because four distinct exposed points cannot have one between two others, and in
any case the total span would exceed `D`.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 2. The collar extension exists and keeps the crosscuts disjoint

The audited topology starts with an embedded closed topological disk `B` and finitely
many pairwise-disjoint proper crosscuts `gamma_i`.  Choose a large Euclidean round disk
`K` with `B subset int(K)` and containing the full finite source.

The region

\[
K\setminus\operatorname{int}B
\]

is an annulus.  By Jordan--Schoenflies / the collar theorem for a disk boundary it is
homeomorphic to `S^1 x [0,1]`, with the two boundary circles corresponding to
`partial B` and `partial K`.  Extending each crosscut endpoint along its distinct
vertical fiber produces two disjoint extension arcs.  Distinct endpoints use distinct
fibers, so extensions belonging to different train crosscuts cannot meet in the
annulus.  Inside `B` the crosscuts were already disjoint.

Thus every `gamma_i` becomes one proper crosscut `tilde gamma_i` of `K`, and the family
remains pairwise disjoint.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 3. The complement-dual tree is unchanged by the collar

A possible failure would be that two different old complement regions merge through
the added annulus.  They cannot.

There are `m` pairwise-disjoint proper crosscuts in `B`, hence exactly `m+1` old
regions and a tree dual with `m` edges.  The extended family consists of `m`
pairwise-disjoint proper crosscuts in the disk `K`, hence also has exactly `m+1`
complement regions and a tree dual with `m` edges.

Each old region is contained in an extended complement component.  Two old regions
separated by `gamma_i` remain on opposite sides of its extension `tilde gamma_i`, so
they cannot lie in the same extended component.  Therefore the map from old regions
to extended components is injective.  Since both sets have `m+1` elements, it is a
bijection preserving which crosscut separates which pair of regions.  The dual trees
are canonically isomorphic.

This counting/separation argument is stronger and cleaner than relying only on the
informal phrase “collar sectors do not merge.”

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 4. Literal diameter chords stay inside the enlarged disk

`K` is chosen convex and contains every source point.  Hence the Euclidean segment
between any two source points lies in `K`, in particular every actual diameter chord.
No retraction, isotopy, or replacement curve is used.  Pairwise chord intersections
from Section 1 therefore remain literal common Euclidean points in the same ambient
disk used for the dual-cell traces.

This is why the collar extension repairs the original nonconvex-disk gap without
weakening the geometric-thrackle input.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 5. Cell-closure traces are connected subtrees even with repeated crossings

For one connected curve `s`, let `R(s)` be the set of extended complement cells whose
closures meet `s`.  Take two cells in `R(s)` and an edge of the unique dual path between
them.  The associated proper crosscut separates the two cell unions.  A connected set
meeting both closures must meet the separator.  At that separator point, both incident
cell closures meet `s`.  Repeating along every path edge puts the whole dual path in
`R(s)`.

Nothing in this proof assumes that `s` crosses a train crosscut once.  If `s` is
tangent to a crosscut, overlaps it, or crosses it repeatedly, closure-based membership
only enlarges `R(s)` while preserving connectedness.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 6. Pairwise trace intersection is not lost at crosscut points

Two diameter chords meet at a Euclidean point `p`.  If `p` lies in an open extended
cell, that cell is plainly in both trace subtrees.  If `p` lies on one or more extended
crosscuts or on the ambient boundary, choose any incident extended cell.  Its closure
contains `p`, so it belongs to both trace subtrees.

Thus the trace subtrees are genuinely pairwise intersecting even at all boundary and
separator degeneracies.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 7. The tree Helly proof is correct

Connected subgraphs of a tree are convex in the graph metric.  The candidate gives a
self-contained proof: root the tree; for each subtree choose its nearest-root vertex;
select the subtree whose such vertex has maximum depth.  Pairwise intersection with
that subtree forces its nearest-root vertex to lie on the path between the other
subtree's nearest-root vertex and a common point, hence inside the other subtree.

Therefore all finite diameter trace subtrees share one dual vertex `R_*`.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 8. The conclusion is an extended-cell statement, not an original-strip statement

This distinction is essential.

The common dual vertex labels both an original train-disk region and its collar-enlarged
cell.  The theorem proves that every straight diameter segment meets the closure of the
**extended** cell.  The meeting point may lie in the collar outside the original train
disk.  Therefore the theorem does not yet put all diameter chords through one bounded
rhombic strip or one small Euclidean neighborhood.

The proof note now states this explicitly.  Any downstream metric use must either:

1. show that the relevant common-cell intersections occur inside the original train
   disk; or
2. control the geometry of the collar portion for the selected backbone.

No such metric claim is silently imported here.

**Verdict:** PASS with stated limitation.

---

## 9. Projection to a backbone is topologically safe

Let `P` be a dual-tree path and `Q_*` the tree projection of `R_*` to `P`.  If one
actual diameter chord has nonzero shore incidence on a selected cut, its endpoints lie
on opposite sides of the corresponding extended proper crosscut.  The connected
straight chord meets that crosscut, so its trace subtree meets `P`.  Since the trace
subtree also contains `R_*`, connectedness forces it to contain `Q_*`.

Thus every active diameter row has a straight geometric trace meeting the closure of
the same extended-cell label `Q_*`.

This still does **not** imply that its endpoint-region support interval contains
`Q_*`.  Repeated geometric crossings can create trace branches outside the minimal
endpoint path.  The candidate explicitly refuses that stronger inference.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## Final disposition

After repairing the nonconvex-disk issue, the theorem is valid at its stated exact
finite scope:

```text
pairwise-disjoint proper O-O crosscuts
+ planar diameter-chord thrackle
+ convex collar extension preserving the same dual tree
+ subtree Helly
=
one common extended dual cell met by every actual diameter chord.
```

On the live `0.017104D-o(D)` backbone, all shore-active diameter rows additionally
have trace subtrees through the projection of that common cell to the backbone.  This
is new structural information but not yet a C-II contradiction.

**Final verdict:** PASS. C-II and Level I remain open.
